{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi3zS6gjpR4jeyfW2qPU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TYi87tyk6aGSoxWpeuM72J","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261104-073322__xcf3323/work/PLAN.md","limit":520},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1931,"cache_read_input_tokens":22345,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1931},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"383f0dbc-f521-4dcc-a5c1-4ea066af957b","uuid":"a5ee88a6-ec06-4230-88ee-55268f2cd3a5","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:33:28.870Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi3zRnqDjjAUpg54i9WM","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01TYi87tyk6aGSoxWpeuM72J":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261104-073322__xcf3323/work/PLAN.md","limit":520}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TYi87tyk6aGSoxWpeuM72J","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# EUROPHORIA FOOTBALL\n2\t\n3\t**Over-the-top arcade football. The build plan for TASK.md.**\n4\t\n5\tThe title was picked the way TASK.md asks: 50 riffs on \"European football\" were brainstormed, then Python's\n6\t`secrets.randbelow(50)` (OS entropy, not a seeded generator) chose index 0, **Europhoria Football** (Euro + euphoria).\n7\tUse exactly this title everywhere: the title screen, `<title>`, the About screen, the AI Hub card, the exit interview.\n8\tDo not pick again.\n9\t\n10\t| | |\n11\t|---|---|\n12\t| **In one line** | A broke seaside club gets into the continent's biggest cup because of a typo, and its fans' euphoria carries it to the final. It plays as 5-a-side arcade football with super strikes, slide tackles that send people flying, dives, VAR, and a crowd that is your power bar. |\n13\t| **Genre** | Arcade sports: 5-a-side football (4 outfield players and a keeper a side) with a story-driven cup run. 1 to 4 local players; online versus through AI Hub. |\n14\t| **Perspective** | A TV broadcast camera: side-on, raised 40° (a custom engine `View`), scrolling with play. Classic three-quarter (55°) and Aerial (80°) are options in Settings. Replays use turned angles. |\n15\t| **Engine** | my-3D2dge v0.85.2, the handoff package (`dist/handoff` of github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge), full engine, changed only as §5 lists. |\n16\t| **Online** | AI Hub Session Protocol v1 (github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub `lib/hub`), mode `shared-world`, 2–4 players. |\n17\t| **Work root** | The folder that holds TASK.md, STAGE.md and this PLAN.md. |\n18\t| **Dev folder** | `EurophoriaFootball-EuropeanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174834/` inside the work root. All dev files go here. |\n19\t| **Deliverable** | `EurophoriaFootball-EuropeanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174834.html` in the work root: one file, everything inlined, runs from `file://`. |\n20\t| **Exit interview** | `exit-interview.json` in the work root, next to the HTML, in TASK.md's exact shape. |\n21\t\n22\tWho this is for: the developers (people or AI models) who build the game from TASK.md and this file alone. Where this\n23\tplan and TASK.md seem to disagree, TASK.md wins. Where this plan gives a number, it is a starting value for tuning in\n24\tdev mode, not a law.\n25\t\n26\t---\n27\t\n28\t## Contents\n29\t\n30\t1. [The game in one page](#1-the-game-in-one-page)\n31\t2. [Controls, prompts and devices](#2-controls-prompts-and-devices)\n32\t3. [Tech stack, folder layout, build](#3-tech-stack-folder-layout-build)\n33\t4. [Architecture](#4-architecture)\n34\t5. [Engine changes](#5-engine-changes)\n35\t6. [Systems and mechanics](#6-systems-and-mechanics)\n36\t7. [Content](#7-content)\n37\t8. [Art and animation](#8-art-and-animation)\n38\t9. [Build order and milestones](#9-build-order-and-milestones)\n39\t10. [Risks and how to de-risk them](#10-risks-and-how-to-de-risk-them)\n40\t11. [Testing](#11-testing)\n41\t12. [Definition of done and hand-back](#12-definition-of-done-and-hand-back)\n42\t- [Appendix A: tuning defaults (dev sliders)](#appendix-a-tuning-defaults-dev-sliders)\n43\t- [Appendix B: network messages](#appendix-b-network-messages)\n44\t- [Appendix C: data shapes](#appendix-c-data-shapes)\n45\t- [Appendix D: music sketches](#appendix-d-music-sketches)\n46\t\n47\t---\n48\t\n49\t## 1. The game in one page\n50\t\n51\t### 1.1 Core loop\n52\t\n53\t> Win the ball, pass it about, beat your man, hit it like a train, and keep the crowd fed until they lift you into\n54\t> EUROPHORIA.\n55\t\n56\tEach match: possession, then chances, then the crowd's Euphoria rises, then a Europhoria burst and a Super Strike,\n57\tthen a goal, a celebration and a replay, then the kickoff again. Each cup round: a tabloid back page, a team talk, the\n58\tmatch, the result, then the next stop on the map.\n59\t\n60\t### 1.2 Pillars\n61\t\n62\t1. **Football first, circus second.** Passing, shooting, tackling and keeping must feel good with every over-the-top\n63\t   (OTT) extra switched off (dev mode has a \"pure football\" toggle). The OTT layer rewards good football; it never\n64\t   replaces it.\n65\t2. **The crowd is the power bar.** Euphoria is not an abstract meter: it is the noise of your fans. They chant, wave\n66\t   scarves, light flares and unfurl the tifo as it fills.\n67\t3. **Broadcast drama.** It is framed like football on TV: a side-on camera, a score bug, two commentators, replays,\n68\t   a fourth official's board, VAR.\n69\t4. **Couch and TV first.** A DualSense at 10 feet is the reference device. Big type, few choices per screen, glyph\n70\t   prompts, every screen usable with the d-pad and ✕/○. Keyboard and mouse are native too.\n71\t5. **Everything is data.** Teams, stadiums, story, commentary, drills, events, supers, chants and songs are entries in\n72\t   registries, so the rest of the game can be built out by adding data (§4.6).\n73\t\n74\t### 1.3 From the source to the mechanics\n75\t\n76\t| What makes European football itself | What it becomes in the game |\n77\t|---|---|\n78\t| Terraces, chants, scarves, flares, tifos | The Euphoria meter (§6.9), crowd states and chants (§6.18), the tifo and flares at Europhoria |\n79\t| Tactical schools: route one, tiki-taka, gegenpressing, catenaccio, galácticos | Each opponent's AI profile and gimmick (§6.13, §7.1); \"parking the bus\" is literal |\n80\t| Cold rainy nights, snow, the orange ball, floodlights | Weather that changes ball physics and visibility (§6.19) |\n81\t| Dives, cards, the referee, VAR | A Theatrics (dive) button, referee strictness, cards, advantage, VAR checks (§6.10) |\n82\t| Cup magic, giant-killing underdogs | The Cup Run story (§6.24, §7.3) |\n83\t| Tabloid back pages, commentary clichés, punditry | Tabloid screens between matches, two-voice commentary (§6.17, §7.4) |\n84\t| Sticker albums | The Sticker Album (§6.31, §7.5) |\n85\t| Goal celebrations | A celebration picker after every goal (§6.21) |\n86\t| Penalty heartbreak | Shootouts, the Panenka (§6.11, §6.12) |\n87\t| Nutmegs, step-overs, rainbow flicks, bicycle kicks, diving headers | Skill moves and the air-ball moves (§6.5, §6.6) |\n88\t| Action replays | Goal replays from turned camera angles (§6.15) |\n89\t| Pitch invaders, seagulls, beach balls on the pitch, floodlight failures | Matchday events (§6.20) |\n90\t\n91\t### 1.4 How long the demo is, and where each mechanic shows up first\n92\t\n93\tAt the default half length (2:00 of real time per half) a cup match takes about 6 minutes. The full demo is about 45–60\n94\tminutes: the cup run (5 matches and their story beats), 9 training drills, Kick-About, Penalties, the Sticker Album, and\n95\tthe attract mode. Every mechanic is required at some point in the cup run, in this order:\n96\t\n97\t| Mechanic | Taught in training drill | Required first in |\n98\t|---|---|---|\n99\t| Run, sprint, stamina, pass, shoot, power, aim | 1, 2, 3 | Qualifying Round (Hoofington) |\n100\t| Lobs, crosses, headers, volleys, corners, goal kicks | 4 | Qualifying Round (Hoofington are all about the air) |\n101\t| Tackles, slides, barges, switching, interceptions | 6 | Round of 16 (Pasapasa keep the ball) |\n102\t| Through balls, one-twos | 2 | Round of 16 |\n103\t| Skill moves, nutmegs; snow physics, the orange ball | 5 | Quarter-final (Stahlpresse press you) |\n104\t| Free kicks, penalties, cards, advantage, dives, VAR, the bus | 7, 9 | Semi-final (Lucchetto) |\n105\t| Euphoria, Europhoria, Super Strikes, the Save Duel | 8 | All matches; the final requires a Super Strike for its challenge |\n106\t| Golden goal, penalty shootout | 7 | Any drawn cup tie; Penalties mode; the attract mode shows one |\n107\t| Celebrations, replays, matchday events, weather | none | All matches |\n108\t\n109\t---\n110\t\n111\t## 2. Controls, prompts and devices\n112\t\n113\t### 2.1 Action table\n114\t\n115\tThe reference pad is the PS5 DualSense in the browser's standard mapping (engine codes `Pad0`..`Pad16`). Prompts show\n116\tPlayStation glyphs by default; Settings → Controller → Button symbols switches to Xbox (A B X Y).\n117\t\n118\t| Action id | With the ball | Without the ball | Pad (PS / Xbox) | Keyboard | Mouse (in play) |\n119\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n120\t| move | run / dribble | run | L-stick, d-pad | WASD, arrows | — |\n121\t| `sprint` | sprint (stamina) | sprint | R2 / RT (`Pad7`) | Left Shift | — |\n122\t| `pass` | ground pass to the teammate the stick points at | standing tackle (poke) | ✕ / A (`Pad0`) | J | Left click: pass toward the cursor (click a teammate to pass to them) |\n123\t| `shoot` | hold to charge, release to shoot; stick aims | slide tackle | □ / X (`Pad2`) | K | Right button held: charge, release to shoot at the cursor |\n124\t| `lob` | lob, cross from wide, chip near the keeper | shoulder barge | ○ / B (`Pad1`) | L | Middle click: lob toward the cursor |\n125\t| `through` | through ball into space | switch player | △ / Y (`Pad3`) | I | — |\n126\t| `skill` | skill move (by stick direction, §6.6) | — | R1 / RB (`Pad5`) | E | — |\n127\t| `switch` | hold with pass: one-two | switch to the teammate nearest the ball | L1 / LB (`Pad4`) | Q | Left click on a teammate while defending: switch to them |\n128\t| `dive` | Theatrics (dive) | Theatrics | L2 / LT (`Pad6`) | C | — |\n129\t| `euphoria` | trigger Europhoria when the meter is full | same | L1 + R1 together | Space (also Q + E) | — |\n130\t| pause | pause menu (engine) | | Options (`Pad9`) | Esc, P | the corner's menu button |\n131\t| confirm / cancel | menus (engine) | | ✕ / ○ | Enter, Space / Esc, Backspace | click / right click |\n132\t\n133\t- **Mouse aim (keyboard and mouse players).** When the mouse moves over the canvas in play, mouse aim switches on: a\n134\t  pixel reticle sits on the pitch under the pointer (`game.mouseGround()`). Passes, shots and lobs then aim at it, and\n135\t  moving still uses WASD. It switches off after 3 s without mouse movement, or when the stick is used. The wheel zooms\n136\t  the camera (the engine's own player zoom). In every menu the pointer's hover moves the focus and a click picks.\n137\t- **Celebrations and the Save Duel** reuse the face buttons (✕ □ ○ △ / J K L I).\n138\t- **Keepers** are AI-controlled except in penalties (§6.11) and the Save Duel (§6.9). After a catch, the human side's\n139\t  keeper waits up to 2 s for ✕ (throw toward the stick) or □ (kick long), then the AI distributes.\n140\t\n141\t### 2.2 Input preset\n142\t\n143\tDefine one preset, `EF.KEYS`, as a **fresh object**: first copy `up, down, left, right, start, pause, confirm, cancel`\n144\tfrom `E.Input.DEFAULT`, then add the game's actions **in this order**: `pass, shoot, lob, through, sprint, skill,\n145\tswitch, dive, euphoria` (the SPORTS kit's `Object.assign({}, E.Input.DEFAULT, {...})` would keep DEFAULT's own `skill`\n146\tkey in its old place and break the order). The order matters: `Input.halfKeys` gives two players sharing one keyboard\n147\t8 buttons each in declaration order (left half F G H R T Y V B, right half . / , L ; ' K M, plus numpad 1–8). The\n148\tninth action, `euphoria`, gets no half-keyboard key, so on a shared keyboard Europhoria is the `switch` + `skill` chord\n149\t(the same chord as L1 + R1). Detect the chord in the controller: both down within 120 ms. A test checks the halves'\n150\tmapping (§11.3).\n151\t\n152\tMouse buttons: bind `Mouse0` to `pass`, `Mouse2` to `shoot`, `Mouse1` to `lob`, but read them only while mouse aim is\n153\ton, so a click on the canvas cannot fire a pass from a gamepad session.\n154\t\n155\t### 2.3 Prompts and glyphs\n156\t\n157\t- Prompts follow `game.input.lastDevice` (`'keyboard' | 'mouse' | 'gamepad' | 'touch'`), per player\n158\t  (`game.players[i].lastDevice`).\n159\t- **Never pass pad symbols to `E.font`.** The pixel font is ASCII plus a few symbols and draws `'?'` for ✕ ○ □ △. Build\n160\t  `EF.Glyphs` (§4.2, `02-glyphs.js`): pixel-art icons drawn by code, 12×12 at scale 1 (24×24 at scale 2):\n161\t  - PlayStation: ✕ blue `#6aa0ff`, ○ red `#ff5a5a`, □ pink `#ff7ad0`, △ green `#3ad6a0` on a dark disc; L1 R1 L2 R2 as\n162\t    rounded pills; Options and Create as small capsules; L and R sticks as discs with an arrow.\n163\t  - Xbox: A green, B red, X blue, Y yellow discs with the letter; LB RB LT RT pills; Menu, View.\n164\t  - Keyboard: a keycap with `input.codeLabel(code)` (W, Shift, Space, J…), widened to fit.\n165\t  - Mouse: a mouse outline with the left, right or middle button lit, and a wheel.\n166\t- `EF.prompt(r, x, y, action, label, { player, scale })` draws the icon for the action's binding on the player's\n167\t  device, then the label. `EF.promptWidth(...)` is there for centring. The binding comes from the input's map after the\n168\t  player's rebinding: take the first pad code or key code for the action (the logic of `input.label()`), then map it to\n169\t  an icon.\n170\t- **Default PlayStation.** In `new E.Game({ menu: { settings: [...] } })`, redefine the standard `glyphs` setting (a\n171\t  game's own settings are defined after the standard ones and replace a spec with the same key):\n172\t  `{ key: 'glyphs', group: 'Controller', label: 'Button symbols', type: 'select', value: 'playstation', options: [['playstation', 'PlayStation'], ['xbox', 'Xbox']], apply: (g, v) => { g.input.glyphs = v; for (const p of g.players) p.glyphs = v; } }`.\n173\t  Check that the Controls screen shows it under Controller. If it does not, change `STANDARD_SETTINGS.glyphs` in the\n174\t  engine instead (§5).\n175\t- Prompts on screen: context prompts near the controlled player (for example \"□ VOLLEY\" in the air-ball window, \"L1+R1\n176\t  EUROPHORIA!\" when the meter is full), set-piece prompts in a bottom bar, menu prompts in a footer\n177\t  (\"✕ SELECT  ○ BACK  L1/R1 PAGE\"). Settings → Game → Prompts: Always / While learning (the default: a prompt shows\n178\t  until the player has used that action 3 times) / Off.\n179\t\n180\t### 2.4 Shared keyboard, controllers joining\n181\t\n182\tKick-About's setup screen calls `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })`: Start (Options) on a free pad, or 2 on the keyboard\n183\t(player 2 takes the right half; player 1 moves to the left half by itself). Each player's icon then moves left (HOME),\n184\tcentre (CPU, which means not playing) or right (AWAY) with the stick or d-pad, and ✕ marks ready (§6.27). In the cup run,\n185\textra players can join the Kippers for co-op the same way on the pre-match team-talk screen.\n186\t\n187\t### 2.5 Touch-only devices\n188\t\n189\tThere are no touch controls. Every scene sets `touch: []` (no touch buttons), and `input.touchEnabled = false` (no\n190\ttouch stick); check on a phone-sized `tools/check.mjs` run that nothing touch-related is drawn. On a\n191\tdevice with a coarse pointer, no fine pointer and no gamepad, the title shows a panel: \"CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD\n192\tREQUIRED\". Under it is one big SETTINGS button (a tap opens `game.menu('settings')`), and the engine's corner menu button\n193\tstays. The attract mode still starts after 10 s; a tap goes back to the title. A key press or a gamepad connecting\n194\tclears touch-only mode at once (§6.33).\n195\t\n196\t---\n197\t\n198\t## 3. Tech stack, folder layout, build\n199\t\n200\t### 3.1 Stack\n201\t\n202\t- **Engine:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2, the handoff package: `my-3d2dge.js` (the full engine, readable, every module), the\n203\t  cards (`API.md` read whole, `API-INDEX.md` searched, `AI_GUIDE.md`), `docs/ASSETS.md`, `tools/` (check, filmstrip,\n204\t  replay, asset tools), `kits/sports.js` (the starting point for the match). Get it with a sparse clone into\n205\t  `<dev folder>/.scratch/` (not `/tmp`, §3.4):\n206\t  `git clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git .scratch/my-3d2dge`,\n207\t  then `git -C .scratch/my-3d2dge sparse-checkout set dist/handoff src/mocap tools examples/cmu-lib docs` (about 30 MB).\n208\t  Copy only what is needed into the dev folder, and delete `.scratch/` (with its `.git`) before hand-back, so no nested\n209\t  repository is left in the work root.\n210\t- **Animation clips:** the engine repository's mocap system: `src/mocap/readable.js`, `src/mocap/mocap.js`, the sets\n211\t  `src/mocap/sets/quaternius.js`, `mesh2motion.js`, `cmu.js`, the CMU soccer takes `examples/cmu-lib/CMU_10.js` and\n212\t  `CMU_11.js` (kick soccer ball), and `tools/anim-set.mjs` (with `tools/mocap-lib.mjs`) to cut one small set for the game\n213\t  (§8.3). Licences: Quaternius CC0, Mesh2Motion CC0, CMU free for all uses. Credit them on the About screen.\n214\t- **AI Hub client:** `session.js` 1.4.0 from `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js`,\n215\t  pasted unmodified. Harness for testing: `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` from the same folder.\n216\t- **Language:** plain JavaScript (ES2022) in one IIFE, as the engine's rules require. No libraries, no CDN, no network\n217\t  requests outside the hub client's `postMessage`.\n218\t- **Tools:** Node 24 (on this machine), Playwright 1.63.0 (the version `dist/handoff/package.json` pins), Python 3 for a\n219\t  local server only when the hub harness needs one.\n220\t\n221\t### 3.2 Folder layout (inside the dev folder)\n222\t\n223\t```\n224\tEurophoriaFootball-EuropeanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174834/\n225\t  package.json            # { \"type\": \"module\", devDependencies: { playwright: \"1.63.0\" }, scripts: { build, test } }\n226\t  engine/\n227\t    my-3d2dge.js          # the handoff engine, with the changes in §5 (marked \"// EF:\" at each change)\n228\t    CHANGES-ENGINE.md     # each engine change: where, what, why (feeds the exit interview's engine feedback)\n229\t    API.md API-INDEX.md AI_GUIDE.md ASSETS.md   # copied for reference\n230\t  vendor/\n231\t    session.js            # AI Hub client 1.4.0, unmodified\n232\t    mocap/readable.js mocap.js   # from the engine repo, unmodified\n233\t  mocap/\n234\t    ef-clips.js           # the game's own clip set, written by tools/anim-set.mjs (§8.3)\n235\t  src/                    # the game, as numbered fragments (§4.2); the build wraps them in one IIFE\n236\t  tools/\n237\t    build.mjs             # builds build/game.js, build/index.html (dev page) and the single-file deliverable\n238\t    test.mjs              # THE test command (§11): prints only failures and one count line\n239\t    harness/              # harness.html, bridge.js, round.js from ai-hub lib/hub (for the online test)\n240\t    lib/                  # test helpers: vm loader, fake hub, scripted bots, assertions\n241\t    check.mjs filmstrip.mjs replay.mjs   # copied from the handoff tools/\n242\t  build/                  # generated: game.js, index.html\n243\t  shots/                  # screenshots and filmstrips (generated, for things a script can't check)\n244\t```\n245\t\n246\t### 3.3 Build\n247\t\n248\t`node tools/build.mjs` does four things:\n249\t\n250\t1. **Concatenate** `src/*.js` in file-name order into `build/game.js`, wrapped as\n251\t   `(() => { 'use strict'; const E = My3D2dge, { px, clamp, lerp, approach, TAU } = E; ... })();`. Each fragment is\n252\t   plain code that shares the wrapper's scope (no IIFE of its own). Put `//# sourceURL=` comments per fragment so\n253\t   errors name the file.\n254\t2. **Inject** the About data: read `../exit-interview.json` if it exists (else a stub with \"unknown\" values) and emit\n255\t   `EF.ABOUT = {...}` as the first line inside the IIFE, so the About screen and the exit interview never disagree.\n256\t3. **Write `build/index.html`**: the dev page, which loads `../vendor/session.js` first in `<head>`, then\n257\t   `../engine/my-3d2dge.js`, `../vendor/mocap/readable.js`, `../vendor/mocap/mocap.js`, `../mocap/ef-clips.js`,\n258\t   `game.js`.\n259\t4. **Write the deliverable** `../EurophoriaFootball-EuropeanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174834.html`, with the\n260\t   same scripts inlined in the same order. The `<head>` must be exactly in this order:\n261\t   1. `<meta charset=\"utf-8\">`\n262\t   2. `<script>` + session.js, unmodified + `</script>` (the hub requires it as the first script, right after charset)\n263\t   3. `<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover\">`, `<title>Europhoria Football</title>`, a description meta\n264\t   4. the hub session metas: `hub:session` = `1`; `hub:session-players` = `2-4`; `hub:session-mode` = `shared-world`;\n265\t      `hub:session-minutes` = `4`; `hub:session-goal` = `Outscore the other side before the final whistle`;\n266\t      `hub:session-howto` = `Move: stick/WASD · Pass ✕/J · Shoot □/K (hold) · Lob ○/L · Sprint R2/Shift` (at most 80 characters)\n267\t   5. catalogue metas: `hub:source` = `European football`, `hub:type` = `Game`, `hub:genre` = `Sports`, `hub:players` = `1–4`\n268\t   6. the page style (from `game/index.html` of the handoff: black, full window, `image-rendering: pixelated`)\n269\t\n270\t   The `<body>` holds the boot splash markup (`E.bootSplash('EUROPHORIA FOOTBALL')`, as `game/index.html` has it) and\n271\t   `<canvas id=\"screen\">`, then the engine, mocap, clip set and game scripts. Escape any `</script` inside inlined code\n272\t   as `<\\/script`. Check for it: the build fails if an inlined file contains `</script`.\n273\t\n274\t### 3.4 Environment notes for this machine\n275\t\n276\t- Other agents share this machine. Work only inside the dev folder and the work root's deliverables. Do not read other\n277\t  run folders.\n278\t- `/tmp` (a tmpfs) was full during planning, and Claude Code's own temp folder lives there, so command output can be\n279\t  lost. Redirect long output to files in the dev folder and read those. Set `TMPDIR` to `<dev folder>/.tmp` for npm,\n280\t  git and Playwright.\n281\t- Playwright's Chromium may already be installed (`~/.cache/ms-playwright`). Run `npx playwright install chromium`\n282\t  only if a test says it is missing.\n283\t- Servers: only the hub harness needs one. See §11.6 for the exact way to start it, check it, and stop it by PID.\n284\t\n285\t---\n286\t\n287\t## 4. Architecture\n288\t\n289\t### 4.1 Runtime overview\n290\t\n291\t```\n292\t          ┌──────────── controllers (all produce an Intent each step) ────────────┐\n293\t          │ HumanController(input, mouseAim)  BotController(teamBrain)            │\n294\t          │ RemoteController(net buffer)      ScriptController(attract/training)  │\n295\t          └───────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n296\t                          ▼ Intent per controlled footballer\n297\t ┌───────────────────── Match (simulation, update(dt), world units, seeded RNG) ──────────────────┐\n298\t │ Pitch · Ball · Footballers(10) · Keepers · Referee · Rules (state machine, clock, restarts)    │\n299\t │ Euphoria · SetPieces · TeamBrains(2) · MatchEvents(random) · Stats                             │\n300\t └──────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n301\t                ▼ typed events on EF.bus (goal, shot, pass, tackle, foul, card, whistle, bounce…)\n302\t   Presentation: Camera · Crowd · Commentary · Audio (sfx, music, crowd engine, babble) · HUD ·\n303\t                 Replay recorder · Rumble · Stats/Stickers · Network host (forwards events)\n304\t                ▼ draw(r)\n305\t   MatchView: pitch floor/walls, stands & crowd, goals & nets, actors (rigs), ball, effects, HUD overlay\n306\t```\n307\t\n308\t- **The Match is pure simulation.** It reads Intents, moves bodies, applies rules, and emits events. It never reads\n309\t  input devices, never plays sounds, never draws. Everything else reacts to its events and reads its state. This one\n310\t  rule makes the bots, the attract mode, replays, tests and the network host all work the same way.\n311\t- **Animation runs on the presentation side.** Rigs are updated from each footballer's state (`velocity`, `facing`,\n312\t  `action`, `actionU`, `pose`), not from input. A client on the network does the same from snapshot state.\n313\t- **The fixed step.** The engine runs `update(dt)` at 1/120 s. The sim uses `dt` everywhere and never `Math.random`:\n314\t  it uses `match.rng = E.rng(seed)`. Cosmetic randomness (crowd, particles, voices) uses `EF.fxRng`, a separate stream,\n315\t  so the same seed and inputs give the same match whatever the presentation does.\n316\t\n317\t### 4.2 Modules (src fragments, in load order)\n318\t\n319\t| File | Holds | Key names it exports (on `EF`) |\n320\t|---|---|---|\n321\t| `00-core.js` | the namespace `EF`, registries `EF.def/EF.get/EF.all`, the event bus `EF.bus.on/off/emit`, the tuning table `EF.T` and `EF.tune(key)`, RNG streams, small math helpers, deep-link parsing; then, in this order, the `broadcast` View, the `EF.KEYS` preset (§2.2), the `EF.SETTINGS` list (§6.31), and `new E.Game({...})` (§4.7), because the Game reads all three when it is created | `game`, `EF.def`, `EF.bus`, `EF.T`, `EF.tune`, `EF.fxRng`, `EF.KEYS`, `EF.SETTINGS` |\n322\t| `01-input.js` | `HumanController`, mouse aim, the euphoria chord, the intent recorder (for tests and attract), touch-only detection | `EF.Intent`, `EF.HumanController`, `EF.touchOnly()` |\n323\t| `02-glyphs.js` | glyph icons and `EF.prompt` (§2.3) | `EF.Glyphs`, `EF.prompt` |\n324\t| `03-audio.js` | SFX voices, songs, `EF.Crowd` audio (the crowd engine), `EF.Babble` (gibberish voices), `EF.Chant` (sung chants), rumble helpers; all no-ops headless | `EF.sfx`, `EF.music`, `EF.crowdAudio`, `EF.babble` |\n325\t| `04-art.js` | palettes, kits as rig colours, ball sprites, card/whistle/flag/glove sprites, crest painter, custom `E.SHAPES` (goal frame, corner flag, dugout, floodlight pylon, team bus, trophy, ad board, camera tower, podium) | `EF.kitColors`, `EF.drawCrest`, `EF.BALLS` |\n326\t| `05-anim.js` | the clip library (`Mocap.load(MOCAP.EF)`), `EF.Anim.play(rig, clip, o)`, procedural moves (body tilt recipes, keeper dives, header, bicycle, dive, celebrations), football kick specs | `EF.Anim`, `EF.KICKS` |\n327\t| `10-pitch.js` | pitch geometry constants, the TileMap (stands, boards, aprons, nets), floor textures by stadium (stripes, wear, mud, snow, wet sheen, puddles), lines, out-of-play tests | `EF.Pitch`, `P.X0 P.X1 P.Y0 P.Y1 P.CX P.CY P.GH P.BAR` |\n328\t| `11-ball.js` | ball physics (Body, surfaces, spin/curl, drag, posts and bar, net, boards), possession and dribble touches, ball trails, super-strike flight paths | `EF.Ball` |\n329\t| `12-footballer.js` | the Footballer entity: body, stats, stamina, state machine, action execution (pass, shoot, lob, through, skill, tackle, slide, barge, dive, air moves), contact and knockdowns | `EF.Footballer` |\n330\t| `13-keeper.js` | keeper positioning, shot reading, save decisions, dives, catches, parries, punches, distribution, rushing out | `EF.Keeper` |\n331\t| `14-team-ai.js` | `TeamBrain` (phase, formation spots, pressing, marking, runs), the ball-carrier decision model, set-piece routines, difficulty | `EF.TeamBrain`, `EF.BotController` |\n332\t| `15-rules.js` | the match state machine, the clock (game minutes, stoppage time), restarts, half time, full time, golden goal, shootout bookkeeping | `EF.Rules` |\n333\t| `16-referee.js` | the referee character, foul judgement, advantage, cards, theatrics judgement, VAR sequence, referee personalities | `EF.Referee` |\n334\t| `17-euphoria.js` | per-team Euphoria, gains and decay, Europhoria, Super Strikes (registry `super`), the Save Duel | `EF.Euphoria`, `EF.SaveDuel` |\n335\t| `18-setpieces.js` | kickoff, goal kick, corner, free kick, penalty, shootout controllers and their UIs' state | `EF.SetPiece` |\n336\t| `19-crowd.js` | stand layout, fans (sprites, and classic-style rigs in the front rows), crowd mood, crowd animation states, tifo, flares, Mexican wave, the crowd throw-back | `EF.CrowdView`, `EF.mood` |\n337\t| `20-events.js` | matchday events (registry `event`): seagull, beach ball, pitch invader, floodlight flicker, flare smoke, drone, fireworks | `EF.MatchEvents` |\n338\t| `21-camera.js` | the broadcast camera director (follow, lead, zoom by context, set-piece framing, cinematic moments, replay cameras) | `EF.Camera` |\n339\t| `22-replay.js` | the ring-buffer recorder and the replay player | `EF.Replay` |\n340\t| `23-commentary.js` | the commentary director (event to line pools, priority, cooldowns, pairs, variables), the radio box, the PA announcer | `EF.Commentary` |\n341\t| `24-hud.js` | score bug, clock, Euphoria gauges, name tags, stamina, power meter, reticles, radar, notices, prompts, the Save Duel ring, the fourth official's board | `EF.HUD` |\n342\t| `30-ui.js` | the 10-foot UI kit: a screen stack and widgets (Tile, Button, List, Slider, Toggle, Tabs, Card, Pager) with d-pad, stick, keyboard, mouse hover and click | `EF.UI` |\n343\t| `31-front.js` | scenes: `title`, `setup` (Kick-About), `penaltysetup`, `album`, `about`; the touch-only notice | scenes |\n344\t| `32-cup.js` | scenes: `cup` (bracket and story beat player: tabloid, travel map, talk, team talk, results), `tunnel` (walkout; also the AI Hub card), `ending` (ceremony, parade, credits) | scenes |\n345\t| `33-match.js` | scene `match`: builds a Match from options, runs it, MatchView drawing, half time, results overlay; deep-link parts | scene |\n346\t| `34-training.js` | scene `training`: drill select, the drill runner (registry `drill`) | scene |\n347\t| `40-attract.js` | the idle watcher (10 s on title and menu scenes) and scene `attract` (a scripted showcase, §6.29) | scene |\n348\t| `41-devmode.js` | the dev panel (tabs Tune, Cheats, Warp, Info), the pause-menu entry, F1, tuning persistence and reset | `EF.Dev` |\n349\t| `42-hub.js` | AI Hub sessions: scene `hubwait` (connecting), connect, card, ready, start, host role (sim, snapshots, events), client role (input, interpolation), server mode, leave, time-up, end, finish, the SESSION SETTINGS overlay | `EF.Hub` |\n350\t| `43-saves.js` | the save schema (cup progress, stickers, unlocks, records), `game.saves` hook-up, migrations | `EF.Save` |\n351\t| `50-teams.js` | team, formation, tactic and referee data (§7.1) | data |\n352\t| `51-stadiums.js` | stadium data (§7.2) | data |\n353\t| `52-story.js` | the cup's story beats, tabloids, team talk lines, challenges, stickers (§7.3, §7.5) | data |\n354\t| `53-commentary-lines.js` | the commentary line pools (§7.4) | data |\n355\t| `54-drills.js` | training drills (§7.6) | data |\n356\t| `55-audio-data.js` | songs and chants (§7.8, Appendix D) | data |\n357\t| `90-start.js` | `game.debug`, `EF.test` hooks, boot steps, the content validation run, `game.start({...})` | — |\n358\t\n359\t### 4.3 Core contracts\n360\t\n361\t**Intent** (what a controller asks of one footballer for this step):\n362\t\n363\t```js\n364\t{ move: [x, y],            // ground direction, length 0..1 (screen direction already turned to ground: view.screenDirToGround)\n365\t  aim: [x, y] | null,      // explicit aim (mouse reticle direction) or null (aim with move)\n366\t  aimPoint: {x, y} | null, // the reticle's ground point (mouse), for passes to a spot\n367\t  sprint: false,\n368\t  pass: B, shoot: B, lob: B, through: B, skill: B, sw: B, dive: B,   // B = { down, pressed, released, held (seconds) }\n369\t  euphoria: false,         // pressed this step (the key, or the chord)\n370\t  target: null }           // a teammate picked by a mouse click, or null\n371\t```\n372\t\n373\tEvery controller (human, bot, remote, script) returns one. `Footballer.act(intent, dt)` is the only way a footballer\n374\tdoes anything. Tests drive footballers by feeding Intents through `ScriptController`, or by pressing actions on\n375\t`game.input` (`input.tap('pass')`, `input.hold('shoot')`) through a `HumanController`.\n376\t\n377\t**Footballer state** (what the presentation, replays and the network read):\n378\t\n379\t```js\n380\t{ id, side, role, team, data /* roster entry */, body /* E.Body */, facing, stamina, ctl /* controller */,\n381\t  state: 'run' | 'kick' | 'slide' | 'poke' | 'barge' | 'skill' | 'air' | 'down' | 'stunned' | 'celebrate' | 'dive'\n382\t       | 'keeper-set' | 'keeper-dive' | 'keeper-hold' | 'off' /* sent off */ | 'setpiece',\n383\t  action: null | 'pass' | 'shot' | 'chip' | 'volley' | 'header' | 'bicycle' | 'divingHeader' | 'stepover' | ...,\n384\t  actionT, actionU /* 0..1 */, pose, expr, downT, cards: 0..2, sentOff: false, human: playerIndex | null }\n385\t```\n386\t\n387\t**Match state** (sim): `{ mode, half, clock /* real s */, minute /* game min */, stoppage, score: [a, b], possession,\n388\tlastTouch, euphoria: [a, b], europhoria: { side, t }, setpiece, shootout, cards, stats, rngSeed }`. Modes:\n389\t`'kickoff' | 'play' | 'stoppage' | 'setpiece' | 'goal' | 'celebrate' | 'replay' | 'var' | 'halftime' | 'fulltime' |\n390\t'extratime' | 'shootout' | 'over'`.\n391\t\n392\t**Events on `EF.bus`.** Each is `{ type, t /* match time */, side, who /* footballer id */, x, y, z, ...data }`. Types:\n393\t`kickoff whistle touch pass passDone intercept shot onTarget woodwork save catch parry tip goal ownGoal out corner\n394\tgoalKick crowdThrow tackleWon tackleFailed slide barge knockdown foul advantage card sendOff dive diveWon diveBooked var\n395\tvarDecision penaltyAwarded penaltyTaken shootoutKick skill nutmeg rainbow air header volley bicycle euphoriaGain\n396\teuphoriaFull europhoria superStrike saveDuel saveDuelResult celebrate replayStart replayEnd half fulltime extraTime\n397\tgoldenGoal shootoutStart shootoutEnd event stamina`. Listeners: audio, commentary, crowd, camera, HUD, stats, stickers\n398\tand challenges, replay markers, hub host, rumble.\n399\t\n400\t### 4.4 Scenes and flow\n401\t\n402\t```\n403\tboot ──► (AIHub.active?) ──yes──► hub: connect ─► tunnel (as the hub card) ─► match (session) ─► end screen\n404\t   └──no──► title ◄──────────────────────────────── any input ◄── attract (after 10 s idle on title/menus)\n405\t             ├─ CUP RUN ──► cup (bracket ▸ beats: tabloid ▸ travel ▸ talk ▸ team talk) ─► tunnel ─► match ─►\n406\t             │               cup (result ▸ tabloid ▸ stickers) ─► … ─► final ─► ending (ceremony ▸ parade ▸ credits) ─► title\n407\t             ├─ KICK-ABOUT ─► setup ─► tunnel (short) ─► match ─► results ─► setup | title\n408\t             ├─ PENALTIES ──► penaltysetup ─► match { shootoutOnly } ─► results\n409\t             ├─ TRAINING ───► training (drill list ▸ drill ▸ result)\n410\t             ├─ STICKER ALBUM ► album\n411\t             ├─ OPTIONS ────► game.menu('settings')\n412\t             └─ ABOUT & CREDITS ► about\n413\t```\n414\t\n415\t- Gameplay scenes (`match`, `training`) set `pausable: true` (when not in a hub session). Title, menus, cup screens,\n416\t  results and the attract mode do not.\n417\t- **Deep links** (for tests, `tools/check.mjs` and the dev Warp tab): `#title`, `#attract`, `#setup`, `#match`,\n418\t  `#match/<stadiumId>`, `#match/corner`, `#match/freekick`, `#match/penalty`, `#match/shootout`, `#match/super`,\n419\t  `#match/duel`, `#cup`, `#cup/r1`…`#cup/r5` (r1 the Qualifying Round … r5 the final; `#cup/final` works too),\n420\t  `#ending`, `#training/<drillId>`, `#album`, `#about`, `#dev/anim/<move>` (the animation gallery, §11.7). Queries:\n421\t  `?seed=N` (fixes the match RNG), `?touchonly=1` (forces touch-only mode), `?dev=1` (opens the dev panel at start).\n422\t\n423\t### 4.5 Simulation, presentation and network roles\n424\t\n425\t| Role | Runs the Match sim | Reads local input | Draws | Sends |\n426\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n427\t| Single machine (normal) | yes | yes (1–4 players) | yes | — |\n428\t| Hub host with a player | yes | its own player | yes | snapshots and events |\n429\t| Hub host as server (`AIHub.server`) | yes | no | no (draws nothing) | snapshots and events |\n430\t| Hub client | no: it applies snapshots to a mirror Match used for drawing | its own player | yes | its Intent |\n431\t\n432\tThe MatchView and the rig animation code are the same in all four. Only where the state comes from differs.\n433\t\n434\t### 4.6 Registries: the designers' language\n435\t\n436\t`EF.def(kind, id, spec)` registers content; `EF.get(kind, id)` and `EF.all(kind)` read it. Kinds: `team`, `formation`,\n437\t`tactic`, `referee`, `stadium`, `super`, `celebration`, `event`, `drill`, `beat`, `lines` (commentary pools), `chant`,\n438\t`song`, `sticker`, `challenge`, `ball`, `teamtalk`. Content refers to other content by id only. At boot,\n439\t`EF.validate()` checks every reference (a team's stadium, a player's super, a beat's team and photo, a line pool's\n440\tspeaker) and reports problems through `game.fail` (red box). The test suite runs the same check (§11.3). Adding a team,\n441\ta stadium or a cup round means adding entries, not code. Appendix C shows each shape.\n442\t\n443\t### 4.7 The Game object\n444\t\n445\t```js\n446\t// first: the Game resolves its view, preset and settings when it is created\n447\tE.VIEWS.broadcast = E.VIEWS.broadcast || new E.View('broadcast', 'Broadcast', 0, 40, 1, 1);   // as kits/pinball.js registers its view\n448\tconst session = !!(window.AIHub && AIHub.active);   // session.js ran first (§3.3), so this is known from the first line\n449\tconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: 'broadcast', bg: '#06070c', id: 'europhoria-football',\n450\t  input: EF.KEYS,\n451\t  corner: !session,                         // the hub has its own corner bar\n452\t  menu: {\n453\t    settings: EF.SETTINGS,                  // §6.31: defined in sessions too (gameplay reads game.settings)\n454\t    controls: { pass: 'Pass / Tackle', shoot: 'Shoot / Slide', lob: 'Lob / Barge', through: 'Through ball / Switch',\n455\t                sprint: 'Sprint', skill: 'Skill move', switch: 'Switch / One-two', dive: 'Theatrics', euphoria: 'Europhoria' },\n456\t    items: session ? [] : [['Restart match', () => EF.restartMatch()], ['Dev mode', () => EF.Dev.open()]],\n457\t    quit: session ? false : 'title', saves: false } });\n458\t```\n459\t\n460\tSaves go through `game.saves` from our own menus, not through the pause menu. Theme the engine's standard HTML screens\n461\t(pause, settings, controls) to match the game with `new E.Shell(game, { theme: { ink: '#fff6e0', paper: '#0d1430',\n462\taccent: '#ffc83a', muted: '#9aa6c8', danger: '#ff5a4a', line: '#2a3a6a' } })`, created once in `00-core.js`.\n463\t\n464\t---\n465\t\n466\t## 5. Engine changes\n467\t\n468\tThe engine is ours to change. Keep every change small and marked `// EF:`, and record each one in\n469\t`engine/CHANGES-ENGINE.md` (what, where, why). Planned changes:\n470\t\n471\t1. **Dialog voices that babble (`E.Dialog`).** In `Dialog.update` the box calls `this.game.audio.sfx(this.voice)` on\n472\t   every second newly shown letter (search for `this._blip++`). Change it so that `voice` may be a function: call\n473\t   `voice(ch, index, line)` once for each newly revealed character (including spaces and punctuation, which the\n474\t   function uses for pauses). Allow `say(text, { voice })` to set the voice for that call only, and restore it after.\n475\t   `EF.babble.voiceFor(speakerId)` returns such a function (§6.22.4).\n476\t2. **Shirt patterns and numbers (`E.Humanoid`).** In the shirt branch of the draw code (search for\n477\t   `const t = tones(C.cloth), plate = [shL, shR`), after the base fill, draw the kit pattern clipped to the torso plate,\n478\t   when `o.kit` is set: `{ pattern: 'hoops' | 'stripes' | 'halves' | 'sash' | 'plain', color2, number, numberColor }`.\n479\t   Build hoops as 3 bands: quads from lerps between the shoulder line (shL to shR) and the hip line (hL to hR) at\n480\t   fractions .2–.33, .5–.63, .8–.93. Build stripes as 3 vertical bands from lerps across the plate, halves as the\n481\t   left half of the plate, and a sash as a diagonal band from shL to hR. Use `px.poly` in `tones(color2).base`, and\n482\t   `.sh` on the side away from the light. Draw the number (1 or 2 digits, `E.font` `'tiny'`, `numberColor`) at the\n483\t   torso centre when the rig faces away from the camera (`!facingCam`). Sleeves take the shirt colour (they already do\n484\t   with `sleeves: 'short'`). Fallback if this proves hard: plain shirts in the main colour with `trim` in `color2`, and\n485\t   no numbers.\n486\t3. **Whole-body tilt (`E.Humanoid`).** A generalisation of the built-in roll (`_roll(u)`, applied when state `flip` is\n487\t   set). Add a state `tilt: { axis: 'forward' | 'right', angle, pivot: 'feet' | 'hips', lift }`. After `solvePose()`,\n488\t   rotate every joint in `J` (except `bladeDir`, which only turns) about the pivot by `angle` around the axis in the\n489\t   rig's frame `[forward, right, up]`:\n490\t   - axis `forward` (a sideways fall: keeper dives, sideways tumbles): `r' = r·cos a + u·sin a`, `u' = −r·sin a + u·cos a`;\n491\t   - axis `right` (pitching forward or back: diving headers, bicycle kicks): `f' = f·cos a − u·sin a`, `u' = f·sin a + u·cos a`;\n492\t\n493\t   then raise the whole body by `lift`, and by however much is needed to keep every joint above the floor (as `_roll`\n494\t   does). Set `this.mocapTilt = { body: M, head: M }` (the 3×3 rotation, rows forward/right/up) for that step so the\n495\t   face, hair and belt details tilt with the body (`_offsets` reads it). Move the cape and hair points for this step\n496\t   only and put them back before they simulate, exactly as `_roll` does with `this._unroll`. Keep the built-in `flip`\n497\t   untouched.\n498\t4. **Named audio buses (`Audio`).** Add `audio.bus(name)`: it returns a `GainNode` (created on first use, connected to\n499\t   the master gain) whose volume follows `game.settings[name]` when that setting exists. It returns null when there is\n500\t   no audio context (headless, or sound blocked). We use buses `voices` and `crowd`. Also add `audio.now()` (the\n501\t   context's `currentTime`, or 0) and expose `audio.noiseBuffer()` (the engine's existing noise buffer), so the crowd\n502\t   engine and the babble voice synthesise with the engine's own context, master volume and mute.\n503\t5. **Accented capitals in the 5×7 font.** Add glyphs for `É È Á Ó Ü Ö Ä Ñ Ç` and lower-case `é è á ó ü ö ä ñ ç`, drawn\n504\t   in the font's style (copy the base letter's bitmap and add the mark in the rows above, for capitals in the\n505\t   ascender space). Names and chants use them (\"OLÉ!\", \"ESTADIO PASAPASA\"). Until this lands, write them in ASCII; the\n506\t   content validator warns about any character the font cannot draw.\n507\t6. **Only if needed:** if the standard `glyphs` setting cannot be redefined from the game (§2.3), set\n508\t   `STANDARD_SETTINGS.glyphs.value` to `'playstation'` and its options to PlayStation and Xbox.\n509\t\n510\tDo not change anything else without adding it to `CHANGES-ENGINE.md`.\n511\t\n512\t---\n513\t\n514\t## 6. Systems and mechanics\n515\t\n516\tUnits are world units (1 tile = 16), seconds and radians. `EF.T.<key>` marks a value tuned live in dev mode\n517\t(Appendix A).\n518\t\n519\t### 6.1 Pitch and stadium geometry\n520\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"383f0dbc-f521-4dcc-a5c1-4ea066af957b","uuid":"3cdfb250-2bd1-40f4-8997-bba843a991d5","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:33:29.030Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build__20261104-073322__xcf3323/work/PLAN.md","content":"# EUROPHORIA FOOTBALL\n\n**Over-the-top arcade football. The build plan for TASK.md.**\n\nThe title was picked the way TASK.md asks: 50 riffs on \"European football\" were brainstormed, then Python's\n`secrets.randbelow(50)` (OS entropy, not a seeded generator) chose index 0, **Europhoria Football** (Euro + euphoria).\nUse exactly this title everywhere: the title screen, `<title>`, the About screen, the AI Hub card, the exit interview.\nDo not pick again.\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **In one line** | A broke seaside club gets into the continent's biggest cup because of a typo, and its fans' euphoria carries it to the final. It plays as 5-a-side arcade football with super strikes, slide tackles that send people flying, dives, VAR, and a crowd that is your power bar. |\n| **Genre** | Arcade sports: 5-a-side football (4 outfield players and a keeper a side) with a story-driven cup run. 1 to 4 local players; online versus through AI Hub. |\n| **Perspective** | A TV broadcast camera: side-on, raised 40° (a custom engine `View`), scrolling with play. Classic three-quarter (55°) and Aerial (80°) are options in Settings. Replays use turned angles. |\n| **Engine** | my-3D2dge v0.85.2, the handoff package (`dist/handoff` of github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge), full engine, changed only as §5 lists. |\n| **Online** | AI Hub Session Protocol v1 (github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub `lib/hub`), mode `shared-world`, 2–4 players. |\n| **Work root** | The folder that holds TASK.md, STAGE.md and this PLAN.md. |\n| **Dev folder** | `EurophoriaFootball-EuropeanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174834/` inside the work root. All dev files go here. |\n| **Deliverable** | `EurophoriaFootball-EuropeanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174834.html` in the work root: one file, everything inlined, runs from `file://`. |\n| **Exit interview** | `exit-interview.json` in the work root, next to the HTML, in TASK.md's exact shape. |\n\nWho this is for: the developers (people or AI models) who build the game from TASK.md and this file alone. Where this\nplan and TASK.md seem to disagree, TASK.md wins. Where this plan gives a number, it is a starting value for tuning in\ndev mode, not a law.\n\n---\n\n## Contents\n\n1. [The game in one page](#1-the-game-in-one-page)\n2. [Controls, prompts and devices](#2-controls-prompts-and-devices)\n3. [Tech stack, folder layout, build](#3-tech-stack-folder-layout-build)\n4. [Architecture](#4-architecture)\n5. [Engine changes](#5-engine-changes)\n6. [Systems and mechanics](#6-systems-and-mechanics)\n7. [Content](#7-content)\n8. [Art and animation](#8-art-and-animation)\n9. [Build order and milestones](#9-build-order-and-milestones)\n10. [Risks and how to de-risk them](#10-risks-and-how-to-de-risk-them)\n11. [Testing](#11-testing)\n12. [Definition of done and hand-back](#12-definition-of-done-and-hand-back)\n- [Appendix A: tuning defaults (dev sliders)](#appendix-a-tuning-defaults-dev-sliders)\n- [Appendix B: network messages](#appendix-b-network-messages)\n- [Appendix C: data shapes](#appendix-c-data-shapes)\n- [Appendix D: music sketches](#appendix-d-music-sketches)\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game in one page\n\n### 1.1 Core loop\n\n> Win the ball, pass it about, beat your man, hit it like a train, and keep the crowd fed until they lift you into\n> EUROPHORIA.\n\nEach match: possession, then chances, then the crowd's Euphoria rises, then a Europhoria burst and a Super Strike,\nthen a goal, a celebration and a replay, then the kickoff again. Each cup round: a tabloid back page, a team talk, the\nmatch, the result, then the next stop on the map.\n\n### 1.2 Pillars\n\n1. **Football first, circus second.** Passing, shooting, tackling and keeping must feel good with every over-the-top\n   (OTT) extra switched off (dev mode has a \"pure football\" toggle). The OTT layer rewards good football; it never\n   replaces it.\n2. **The crowd is the power bar.** Euphoria is not an abstract meter: it is the noise of your fans. They chant, wave\n   scarves, light flares and unfurl the tifo as it fills.\n3. **Broadcast drama.** It is framed like football on TV: a side-on camera, a score bug, two commentators, replays,\n   a fourth official's board, VAR.\n4. **Couch and TV first.** A DualSense at 10 feet is the reference device. Big type, few choices per screen, glyph\n   prompts, every screen usable with the d-pad and ✕/○. Keyboard and mouse are native too.\n5. **Everything is data.** Teams, stadiums, story, commentary, drills, events, supers, chants and songs are entries in\n   registries, so the rest of the game can be built out by adding data (§4.6).\n\n### 1.3 From the source to the mechanics\n\n| What makes European football itself | What it becomes in the game |\n|---|---|\n| Terraces, chants, scarves, flares, tifos | The Euphoria meter (§6.9), crowd states and chants (§6.18), the tifo and flares at Europhoria |\n| Tactical schools: route one, tiki-taka, gegenpressing, catenaccio, galácticos | Each opponent's AI profile and gimmick (§6.13, §7.1); \"parking the bus\" is literal |\n| Cold rainy nights, snow, the orange ball, floodlights | Weather that changes ball physics and visibility (§6.19) |\n| Dives, cards, the referee, VAR | A Theatrics (dive) button, referee strictness, cards, advantage, VAR checks (§6.10) |\n| Cup magic, giant-killing underdogs | The Cup Run story (§6.24, §7.3) |\n| Tabloid back pages, commentary clichés, punditry | Tabloid screens between matches, two-voice commentary (§6.17, §7.4) |\n| Sticker albums | The Sticker Album (§6.31, §7.5) |\n| Goal celebrations | A celebration picker after every goal (§6.21) |\n| Penalty heartbreak | Shootouts, the Panenka (§6.11, §6.12) |\n| Nutmegs, step-overs, rainbow flicks, bicycle kicks, diving headers | Skill moves and the air-ball moves (§6.5, §6.6) |\n| Action replays | Goal replays from turned camera angles (§6.15) |\n| Pitch invaders, seagulls, beach balls on the pitch, floodlight failures | Matchday events (§6.20) |\n\n### 1.4 How long the demo is, and where each mechanic shows up first\n\nAt the default half length (2:00 of real time per half) a cup match takes about 6 minutes. The full demo is about 45–60\nminutes: the cup run (5 matches and their story beats), 9 training drills, Kick-About, Penalties, the Sticker Album, and\nthe attract mode. Every mechanic is required at some point in the cup run, in this order:\n\n| Mechanic | Taught in training drill | Required first in |\n|---|---|---|\n| Run, sprint, stamina, pass, shoot, power, aim | 1, 2, 3 | Qualifying Round (Hoofington) |\n| Lobs, crosses, headers, volleys, corners, goal kicks | 4 | Qualifying Round (Hoofington are all about the air) |\n| Tackles, slides, barges, switching, interceptions | 6 | Round of 16 (Pasapasa keep the ball) |\n| Through balls, one-twos | 2 | Round of 16 |\n| Skill moves, nutmegs; snow physics, the orange ball | 5 | Quarter-final (Stahlpresse press you) |\n| Free kicks, penalties, cards, advantage, dives, VAR, the bus | 7, 9 | Semi-final (Lucchetto) |\n| Euphoria, Europhoria, Super Strikes, the Save Duel | 8 | All matches; the final requires a Super Strike for its challenge |\n| Golden goal, penalty shootout | 7 | Any drawn cup tie; Penalties mode; the attract mode shows one |\n| Celebrations, replays, matchday events, weather | none | All matches |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Controls, prompts and devices\n\n### 2.1 Action table\n\nThe reference pad is the PS5 DualSense in the browser's standard mapping (engine codes `Pad0`..`Pad16`). Prompts show\nPlayStation glyphs by default; Settings → Controller → Button symbols switches to Xbox (A B X Y).\n\n| Action id | With the ball | Without the ball | Pad (PS / Xbox) | Keyboard | Mouse (in play) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| move | run / dribble | run | L-stick, d-pad | WASD, arrows | — |\n| `sprint` | sprint (stamina) | sprint | R2 / RT (`Pad7`) | Left Shift | — |\n| `pass` | ground pass to the teammate the stick points at | standing tackle (poke) | ✕ / A (`Pad0`) | J | Left click: pass toward the cursor (click a teammate to pass to them) |\n| `shoot` | hold to charge, release to shoot; stick aims | slide tackle | □ / X (`Pad2`) | K | Right button held: charge, release to shoot at the cursor |\n| `lob` | lob, cross from wide, chip near the keeper | shoulder barge | ○ / B (`Pad1`) | L | Middle click: lob toward the cursor |\n| `through` | through ball into space | switch player | △ / Y (`Pad3`) | I | — |\n| `skill` | skill move (by stick direction, §6.6) | — | R1 / RB (`Pad5`) | E | — |\n| `switch` | hold with pass: one-two | switch to the teammate nearest the ball | L1 / LB (`Pad4`) | Q | Left click on a teammate while defending: switch to them |\n| `dive` | Theatrics (dive) | Theatrics | L2 / LT (`Pad6`) | C | — |\n| `euphoria` | trigger Europhoria when the meter is full | same | L1 + R1 together | Space (also Q + E) | — |\n| pause | pause menu (engine) | | Options (`Pad9`) | Esc, P | the corner's menu button |\n| confirm / cancel | menus (engine) | | ✕ / ○ | Enter, Space / Esc, Backspace | click / right click |\n\n- **Mouse aim (keyboard and mouse players).** When the mouse moves over the canvas in play, mouse aim switches on: a\n  pixel reticle sits on the pitch under the pointer (`game.mouseGround()`). Passes, shots and lobs then aim at it, and\n  moving still uses WASD. It switches off after 3 s without mouse movement, or when the stick is used. The wheel zooms\n  the camera (the engine's own player zoom). In every menu the pointer's hover moves the focus and a click picks.\n- **Celebrations and the Save Duel** reuse the face buttons (✕ □ ○ △ / J K L I).\n- **Keepers** are AI-controlled except in penalties (§6.11) and the Save Duel (§6.9). After a catch, the human side's\n  keeper waits up to 2 s for ✕ (throw toward the stick) or □ (kick long), then the AI distributes.\n\n### 2.2 Input preset\n\nDefine one preset, `EF.KEYS`, as a **fresh object**: first copy `up, down, left, right, start, pause, confirm, cancel`\nfrom `E.Input.DEFAULT`, then add the game's actions **in this order**: `pass, shoot, lob, through, sprint, skill,\nswitch, dive, euphoria` (the SPORTS kit's `Object.assign({}, E.Input.DEFAULT, {...})` would keep DEFAULT's own `skill`\nkey in its old place and break the order). The order matters: `Input.halfKeys` gives two players sharing one keyboard\n8 buttons each in declaration order (left half F G H R T Y V B, right half . / , L ; ' K M, plus numpad 1–8). The\nninth action, `euphoria`, gets no half-keyboard key, so on a shared keyboard Europhoria is the `switch` + `skill` chord\n(the same chord as L1 + R1). Detect the chord in the controller: both down within 120 ms. A test checks the halves'\nmapping (§11.3).\n\nMouse buttons: bind `Mouse0` to `pass`, `Mouse2` to `shoot`, `Mouse1` to `lob`, but read them only while mouse aim is\non, so a click on the canvas cannot fire a pass from a gamepad session.\n\n### 2.3 Prompts and glyphs\n\n- Prompts follow `game.input.lastDevice` (`'keyboard' | 'mouse' | 'gamepad' | 'touch'`), per player\n  (`game.players[i].lastDevice`).\n- **Never pass pad symbols to `E.font`.** The pixel font is ASCII plus a few symbols and draws `'?'` for ✕ ○ □ △. Build\n  `EF.Glyphs` (§4.2, `02-glyphs.js`): pixel-art icons drawn by code, 12×12 at scale 1 (24×24 at scale 2):\n  - PlayStation: ✕ blue `#6aa0ff`, ○ red `#ff5a5a`, □ pink `#ff7ad0`, △ green `#3ad6a0` on a dark disc; L1 R1 L2 R2 as\n    rounded pills; Options and Create as small capsules; L and R sticks as discs with an arrow.\n  - Xbox: A green, B red, X blue, Y yellow discs with the letter; LB RB LT RT pills; Menu, View.\n  - Keyboard: a keycap with `input.codeLabel(code)` (W, Shift, Space, J…), widened to fit.\n  - Mouse: a mouse outline with the left, right or middle button lit, and a wheel.\n- `EF.prompt(r, x, y, action, label, { player, scale })` draws the icon for the action's binding on the player's\n  device, then the label. `EF.promptWidth(...)` is there for centring. The binding comes from the input's map after the\n  player's rebinding: take the first pad code or key code for the action (the logic of `input.label()`), then map it to\n  an icon.\n- **Default PlayStation.** In `new E.Game({ menu: { settings: [...] } })`, redefine the standard `glyphs` setting (a\n  game's own settings are defined after the standard ones and replace a spec with the same key):\n  `{ key: 'glyphs', group: 'Controller', label: 'Button symbols', type: 'select', value: 'playstation', options: [['playstation', 'PlayStation'], ['xbox', 'Xbox']], apply: (g, v) => { g.input.glyphs = v; for (const p of g.players) p.glyphs = v; } }`.\n  Check that the Controls screen shows it under Controller. If it does not, change `STANDARD_SETTINGS.glyphs` in the\n  engine instead (§5).\n- Prompts on screen: context prompts near the controlled player (for example \"□ VOLLEY\" in the air-ball window, \"L1+R1\n  EUROPHORIA!\" when the meter is full), set-piece prompts in a bottom bar, menu prompts in a footer\n  (\"✕ SELECT  ○ BACK  L1/R1 PAGE\"). Settings → Game → Prompts: Always / While learning (the default: a prompt shows\n  until the player has used that action 3 times) / Off.\n\n### 2.4 Shared keyboard, controllers joining\n\nKick-About's setup screen calls `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })`: Start (Options) on a free pad, or 2 on the keyboard\n(player 2 takes the right half; player 1 moves to the left half by itself). Each player's icon then moves left (HOME),\ncentre (CPU, which means not playing) or right (AWAY) with the stick or d-pad, and ✕ marks ready (§6.27). In the cup run,\nextra players can join the Kippers for co-op the same way on the pre-match team-talk screen.\n\n### 2.5 Touch-only devices\n\nThere are no touch controls. Every scene sets `touch: []` (no touch buttons), and `input.touchEnabled = false` (no\ntouch stick); check on a phone-sized `tools/check.mjs` run that nothing touch-related is drawn. On a\ndevice with a coarse pointer, no fine pointer and no gamepad, the title shows a panel: \"CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD\nREQUIRED\". Under it is one big SETTINGS button (a tap opens `game.menu('settings')`), and the engine's corner menu button\nstays. The attract mode still starts after 10 s; a tap goes back to the title. A key press or a gamepad connecting\nclears touch-only mode at once (§6.33).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Tech stack, folder layout, build\n\n### 3.1 Stack\n\n- **Engine:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2, the handoff package: `my-3d2dge.js` (the full engine, readable, every module), the\n  cards (`API.md` read whole, `API-INDEX.md` searched, `AI_GUIDE.md`), `docs/ASSETS.md`, `tools/` (check, filmstrip,\n  replay, asset tools), `kits/sports.js` (the starting point for the match). Get it with a sparse clone into\n  `<dev folder>/.scratch/` (not `/tmp`, §3.4):\n  `git clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git .scratch/my-3d2dge`,\n  then `git -C .scratch/my-3d2dge sparse-checkout set dist/handoff src/mocap tools examples/cmu-lib docs` (about 30 MB).\n  Copy only what is needed into the dev folder, and delete `.scratch/` (with its `.git`) before hand-back, so no nested\n  repository is left in the work root.\n- **Animation clips:** the engine repository's mocap system: `src/mocap/readable.js`, `src/mocap/mocap.js`, the sets\n  `src/mocap/sets/quaternius.js`, `mesh2motion.js`, `cmu.js`, the CMU soccer takes `examples/cmu-lib/CMU_10.js` and\n  `CMU_11.js` (kick soccer ball), and `tools/anim-set.mjs` (with `tools/mocap-lib.mjs`) to cut one small set for the game\n  (§8.3). Licences: Quaternius CC0, Mesh2Motion CC0, CMU free for all uses. Credit them on the About screen.\n- **AI Hub client:** `session.js` 1.4.0 from `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js`,\n  pasted unmodified. Harness for testing: `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` from the same folder.\n- **Language:** plain JavaScript (ES2022) in one IIFE, as the engine's rules require. No libraries, no CDN, no network\n  requests outside the hub client's `postMessage`.\n- **Tools:** Node 24 (on this machine), Playwright 1.63.0 (the version `dist/handoff/package.json` pins), Python 3 for a\n  local server only when the hub harness needs one.\n\n### 3.2 Folder layout (inside the dev folder)\n\n```\nEurophoriaFootball-EuropeanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174834/\n  package.json            # { \"type\": \"module\", devDependencies: { playwright: \"1.63.0\" }, scripts: { build, test } }\n  engine/\n    my-3d2dge.js          # the handoff engine, with the changes in §5 (marked \"// EF:\" at each change)\n    CHANGES-ENGINE.md     # each engine change: where, what, why (feeds the exit interview's engine feedback)\n    API.md API-INDEX.md AI_GUIDE.md ASSETS.md   # copied for reference\n  vendor/\n    session.js            # AI Hub client 1.4.0, unmodified\n    mocap/readable.js mocap.js   # from the engine repo, unmodified\n  mocap/\n    ef-clips.js           # the game's own clip set, written by tools/anim-set.mjs (§8.3)\n  src/                    # the game, as numbered fragments (§4.2); the build wraps them in one IIFE\n  tools/\n    build.mjs             # builds build/game.js, build/index.html (dev page) and the single-file deliverable\n    test.mjs              # THE test command (§11): prints only failures and one count line\n    harness/              # harness.html, bridge.js, round.js from ai-hub lib/hub (for the online test)\n    lib/                  # test helpers: vm loader, fake hub, scripted bots, assertions\n    check.mjs filmstrip.mjs replay.mjs   # copied from the handoff tools/\n  build/                  # generated: game.js, index.html\n  shots/                  # screenshots and filmstrips (generated, for things a script can't check)\n```\n\n### 3.3 Build\n\n`node tools/build.mjs` does four things:\n\n1. **Concatenate** `src/*.js` in file-name order into `build/game.js`, wrapped as\n   `(() => { 'use strict'; const E = My3D2dge, { px, clamp, lerp, approach, TAU } = E; ... })();`. Each fragment is\n   plain code that shares the wrapper's scope (no IIFE of its own). Put `//# sourceURL=` comments per fragment so\n   errors name the file.\n2. **Inject** the About data: read `../exit-interview.json` if it exists (else a stub with \"unknown\" values) and emit\n   `EF.ABOUT = {...}` as the first line inside the IIFE, so the About screen and the exit interview never disagree.\n3. **Write `build/index.html`**: the dev page, which loads `../vendor/session.js` first in `<head>`, then\n   `../engine/my-3d2dge.js`, `../vendor/mocap/readable.js`, `../vendor/mocap/mocap.js`, `../mocap/ef-clips.js`,\n   `game.js`.\n4. **Write the deliverable** `../EurophoriaFootball-EuropeanFootball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174834.html`, with the\n   same scripts inlined in the same order. The `<head>` must be exactly in this order:\n   1. `<meta charset=\"utf-8\">`\n   2. `<script>` + session.js, unmodified + `</script>` (the hub requires it as the first script, right after charset)\n   3. `<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover\">`, `<title>Europhoria Football</title>`, a description meta\n   4. the hub session metas: `hub:session` = `1`; `hub:session-players` = `2-4`; `hub:session-mode` = `shared-world`;\n      `hub:session-minutes` = `4`; `hub:session-goal` = `Outscore the other side before the final whistle`;\n      `hub:session-howto` = `Move: stick/WASD · Pass ✕/J · Shoot □/K (hold) · Lob ○/L · Sprint R2/Shift` (at most 80 characters)\n   5. catalogue metas: `hub:source` = `European football`, `hub:type` = `Game`, `hub:genre` = `Sports`, `hub:players` = `1–4`\n   6. the page style (from `game/index.html` of the handoff: black, full window, `image-rendering: pixelated`)\n\n   The `<body>` holds the boot splash markup (`E.bootSplash('EUROPHORIA FOOTBALL')`, as `game/index.html` has it) and\n   `<canvas id=\"screen\">`, then the engine, mocap, clip set and game scripts. Escape any `</script` inside inlined code\n   as `<\\/script`. Check for it: the build fails if an inlined file contains `</script`.\n\n### 3.4 Environment notes for this machine\n\n- Other agents share this machine. Work only inside the dev folder and the work root's deliverables. Do not read other\n  run folders.\n- `/tmp` (a tmpfs) was full during planning, and Claude Code's own temp folder lives there, so command output can be\n  lost. Redirect long output to files in the dev folder and read those. Set `TMPDIR` to `<dev folder>/.tmp` for npm,\n  git and Playwright.\n- Playwright's Chromium may already be installed (`~/.cache/ms-playwright`). Run `npx playwright install chromium`\n  only if a test says it is missing.\n- Servers: only the hub harness needs one. See §11.6 for the exact way to start it, check it, and stop it by PID.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Architecture\n\n### 4.1 Runtime overview\n\n```\n          ┌──────────── controllers (all produce an Intent each step) ────────────┐\n          │ HumanController(input, mouseAim)  BotController(teamBrain)            │\n          │ RemoteController(net buffer)      ScriptController(attract/training)  │\n          └───────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                          ▼ Intent per controlled footballer\n ┌───────────────────── Match (simulation, update(dt), world units, seeded RNG) ──────────────────┐\n │ Pitch · Ball · Footballers(10) · Keepers · Referee · Rules (state machine, clock, restarts)    │\n │ Euphoria · SetPieces · TeamBrains(2) · MatchEvents(random) · Stats                             │\n └──────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                ▼ typed events on EF.bus (goal, shot, pass, tackle, foul, card, whistle, bounce…)\n   Presentation: Camera · Crowd · Commentary · Audio (sfx, music, crowd engine, babble) · HUD ·\n                 Replay recorder · Rumble · Stats/Stickers · Network host (forwards events)\n                ▼ draw(r)\n   MatchView: pitch floor/walls, stands & crowd, goals & nets, actors (rigs), ball, effects, HUD overlay\n```\n\n- **The Match is pure simulation.** It reads Intents, moves bodies, applies rules, and emits events. It never reads\n  input devices, never plays sounds, never draws. Everything else reacts to its events and reads its state. This one\n  rule makes the bots, the attract mode, replays, tests and the network host all work the same way.\n- **Animation runs on the presentation side.** Rigs are updated from each footballer's state (`velocity`, `facing`,\n  `action`, `actionU`, `pose`), not from input. A client on the network does the same from snapshot state.\n- **The fixed step.** The engine runs `update(dt)` at 1/120 s. The sim uses `dt` everywhere and never `Math.random`:\n  it uses `match.rng = E.rng(seed)`. Cosmetic randomness (crowd, particles, voices) uses `EF.fxRng`, a separate stream,\n  so the same seed and inputs give the same match whatever the presentation does.\n\n### 4.2 Modules (src fragments, in load order)\n\n| File | Holds | Key names it exports (on `EF`) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `00-core.js` | the namespace `EF`, registries `EF.def/EF.get/EF.all`, the event bus `EF.bus.on/off/emit`, the tuning table `EF.T` and `EF.tune(key)`, RNG streams, small math helpers, deep-link parsing; then, in this order, the `broadcast` View, the `EF.KEYS` preset (§2.2), the `EF.SETTINGS` list (§6.31), and `new E.Game({...})` (§4.7), because the Game reads all three when it is created | `game`, `EF.def`, `EF.bus`, `EF.T`, `EF.tune`, `EF.fxRng`, `EF.KEYS`, `EF.SETTINGS` |\n| `01-input.js` | `HumanController`, mouse aim, the euphoria chord, the intent recorder (for tests and attract), touch-only detection | `EF.Intent`, `EF.HumanController`, `EF.touchOnly()` |\n| `02-glyphs.js` | glyph icons and `EF.prompt` (§2.3) | `EF.Glyphs`, `EF.prompt` |\n| `03-audio.js` | SFX voices, songs, `EF.Crowd` audio (the crowd engine), `EF.Babble` (gibberish voices), `EF.Chant` (sung chants), rumble helpers; all no-ops headless | `EF.sfx`, `EF.music`, `EF.crowdAudio`, `EF.babble` |\n| `04-art.js` | palettes, kits as rig colours, ball sprites, card/whistle/flag/glove sprites, crest painter, custom `E.SHAPES` (goal frame, corner flag, dugout, floodlight pylon, team bus, trophy, ad board, camera tower, podium) | `EF.kitColors`, `EF.drawCrest`, `EF.BALLS` |\n| `05-anim.js` | the clip library (`Mocap.load(MOCAP.EF)`), `EF.Anim.play(rig, clip, o)`, procedural moves (body tilt recipes, keeper dives, header, bicycle, dive, celebrations), football kick specs | `EF.Anim`, `EF.KICKS` |\n| `10-pitch.js` | pitch geometry constants, the TileMap (stands, boards, aprons, nets), floor textures by stadium (stripes, wear, mud, snow, wet sheen, puddles), lines, out-of-play tests | `EF.Pitch`, `P.X0 P.X1 P.Y0 P.Y1 P.CX P.CY P.GH P.BAR` |\n| `11-ball.js` | ball physics (Body, surfaces, spin/curl, drag, posts and bar, net, boards), possession and dribble touches, ball trails, super-strike flight paths | `EF.Ball` |\n| `12-footballer.js` | the Footballer entity: body, stats, stamina, state machine, action execution (pass, shoot, lob, through, skill, tackle, slide, barge, dive, air moves), contact and knockdowns | `EF.Footballer` |\n| `13-keeper.js` | keeper positioning, shot reading, save decisions, dives, catches, parries, punches, distribution, rushing out | `EF.Keeper` |\n| `14-team-ai.js` | `TeamBrain` (phase, formation spots, pressing, marking, runs), the ball-carrier decision model, set-piece routines, difficulty | `EF.TeamBrain`, `EF.BotController` |\n| `15-rules.js` | the match state machine, the clock (game minutes, stoppage time), restarts, half time, full time, golden goal, shootout bookkeeping | `EF.Rules` |\n| `16-referee.js` | the referee character, foul judgement, advantage, cards, theatrics judgement, VAR sequence, referee personalities | `EF.Referee` |\n| `17-euphoria.js` | per-team Euphoria, gains and decay, Europhoria, Super Strikes (registry `super`), the Save Duel | `EF.Euphoria`, `EF.SaveDuel` |\n| `18-setpieces.js` | kickoff, goal kick, corner, free kick, penalty, shootout controllers and their UIs' state | `EF.SetPiece` |\n| `19-crowd.js` | stand layout, fans (sprites, and classic-style rigs in the front rows), crowd mood, crowd animation states, tifo, flares, Mexican wave, the crowd throw-back | `EF.CrowdView`, `EF.mood` |\n| `20-events.js` | matchday events (registry `event`): seagull, beach ball, pitch invader, floodlight flicker, flare smoke, drone, fireworks | `EF.MatchEvents` |\n| `21-camera.js` | the broadcast camera director (follow, lead, zoom by context, set-piece framing, cinematic moments, replay cameras) | `EF.Camera` |\n| `22-replay.js` | the ring-buffer recorder and the replay player | `EF.Replay` |\n| `23-commentary.js` | the commentary director (event to line pools, priority, cooldowns, pairs, variables), the radio box, the PA announcer | `EF.Commentary` |\n| `24-hud.js` | score bug, clock, Euphoria gauges, name tags, stamina, power meter, reticles, radar, notices, prompts, the Save Duel ring, the fourth official's board | `EF.HUD` |\n| `30-ui.js` | the 10-foot UI kit: a screen stack and widgets (Tile, Button, List, Slider, Toggle, Tabs, Card, Pager) with d-pad, stick, keyboard, mouse hover and click | `EF.UI` |\n| `31-front.js` | scenes: `title`, `setup` (Kick-About), `penaltysetup`, `album`, `about`; the touch-only notice | scenes |\n| `32-cup.js` | scenes: `cup` (bracket and story beat player: tabloid, travel map, talk, team talk, results), `tunnel` (walkout; also the AI Hub card), `ending` (ceremony, parade, credits) | scenes |\n| `33-match.js` | scene `match`: builds a Match from options, runs it, MatchView drawing, half time, results overlay; deep-link parts | scene |\n| `34-training.js` | scene `training`: drill select, the drill runner (registry `drill`) | scene |\n| `40-attract.js` | the idle watcher (10 s on title and menu scenes) and scene `attract` (a scripted showcase, §6.29) | scene |\n| `41-devmode.js` | the dev panel (tabs Tune, Cheats, Warp, Info), the pause-menu entry, F1, tuning persistence and reset | `EF.Dev` |\n| `42-hub.js` | AI Hub sessions: scene `hubwait` (connecting), connect, card, ready, start, host role (sim, snapshots, events), client role (input, interpolation), server mode, leave, time-up, end, finish, the SESSION SETTINGS overlay | `EF.Hub` |\n| `43-saves.js` | the save schema (cup progress, stickers, unlocks, records), `game.saves` hook-up, migrations | `EF.Save` |\n| `50-teams.js` | team, formation, tactic and referee data (§7.1) | data |\n| `51-stadiums.js` | stadium data (§7.2) | data |\n| `52-story.js` | the cup's story beats, tabloids, team talk lines, challenges, stickers (§7.3, §7.5) | data |\n| `53-commentary-lines.js` | the commentary line pools (§7.4) | data |\n| `54-drills.js` | training drills (§7.6) | data |\n| `55-audio-data.js` | songs and chants (§7.8, Appendix D) | data |\n| `90-start.js` | `game.debug`, `EF.test` hooks, boot steps, the content validation run, `game.start({...})` | — |\n\n### 4.3 Core contracts\n\n**Intent** (what a controller asks of one footballer for this step):\n\n```js\n{ move: [x, y],            // ground direction, length 0..1 (screen direction already turned to ground: view.screenDirToGround)\n  aim: [x, y] | null,      // explicit aim (mouse reticle direction) or null (aim with move)\n  aimPoint: {x, y} | null, // the reticle's ground point (mouse), for passes to a spot\n  sprint: false,\n  pass: B, shoot: B, lob: B, through: B, skill: B, sw: B, dive: B,   // B = { down, pressed, released, held (seconds) }\n  euphoria: false,         // pressed this step (the key, or the chord)\n  target: null }           // a teammate picked by a mouse click, or null\n```\n\nEvery controller (human, bot, remote, script) returns one. `Footballer.act(intent, dt)` is the only way a footballer\ndoes anything. Tests drive footballers by feeding Intents through `ScriptController`, or by pressing actions on\n`game.input` (`input.tap('pass')`, `input.hold('shoot')`) through a `HumanController`.\n\n**Footballer state** (what the presentation, replays and the network read):\n\n```js\n{ id, side, role, team, data /* roster entry */, body /* E.Body */, facing, stamina, ctl /* controller */,\n  state: 'run' | 'kick' | 'slide' | 'poke' | 'barge' | 'skill' | 'air' | 'down' | 'stunned' | 'celebrate' | 'dive'\n       | 'keeper-set' | 'keeper-dive' | 'keeper-hold' | 'off' /* sent off */ | 'setpiece',\n  action: null | 'pass' | 'shot' | 'chip' | 'volley' | 'header' | 'bicycle' | 'divingHeader' | 'stepover' | ...,\n  actionT, actionU /* 0..1 */, pose, expr, downT, cards: 0..2, sentOff: false, human: playerIndex | null }\n```\n\n**Match state** (sim): `{ mode, half, clock /* real s */, minute /* game min */, stoppage, score: [a, b], possession,\nlastTouch, euphoria: [a, b], europhoria: { side, t }, setpiece, shootout, cards, stats, rngSeed }`. Modes:\n`'kickoff' | 'play' | 'stoppage' | 'setpiece' | 'goal' | 'celebrate' | 'replay' | 'var' | 'halftime' | 'fulltime' |\n'extratime' | 'shootout' | 'over'`.\n\n**Events on `EF.bus`.** Each is `{ type, t /* match time */, side, who /* footballer id */, x, y, z, ...data }`. Types:\n`kickoff whistle touch pass passDone intercept shot onTarget woodwork save catch parry tip goal ownGoal out corner\ngoalKick crowdThrow tackleWon tackleFailed slide barge knockdown foul advantage card sendOff dive diveWon diveBooked var\nvarDecision penaltyAwarded penaltyTaken shootoutKick skill nutmeg rainbow air header volley bicycle euphoriaGain\neuphoriaFull europhoria superStrike saveDuel saveDuelResult celebrate replayStart replayEnd half fulltime extraTime\ngoldenGoal shootoutStart shootoutEnd event stamina`. Listeners: audio, commentary, crowd, camera, HUD, stats, stickers\nand challenges, replay markers, hub host, rumble.\n\n### 4.4 Scenes and flow\n\n```\nboot ──► (AIHub.active?) ──yes──► hub: connect ─► tunnel (as the hub card) ─► match (session) ─► end screen\n   └──no──► title ◄──────────────────────────────── any input ◄── attract (after 10 s idle on title/menus)\n             ├─ CUP RUN ──► cup (bracket ▸ beats: tabloid ▸ travel ▸ talk ▸ team talk) ─► tunnel ─► match ─►\n             │               cup (result ▸ tabloid ▸ stickers) ─► … ─► final ─► ending (ceremony ▸ parade ▸ credits) ─► title\n             ├─ KICK-ABOUT ─► setup ─► tunnel (short) ─► match ─► results ─► setup | title\n             ├─ PENALTIES ──► penaltysetup ─► match { shootoutOnly } ─► results\n             ├─ TRAINING ───► training (drill list ▸ drill ▸ result)\n             ├─ STICKER ALBUM ► album\n             ├─ OPTIONS ────► game.menu('settings')\n             └─ ABOUT & CREDITS ► about\n```\n\n- Gameplay scenes (`match`, `training`) set `pausable: true` (when not in a hub session). Title, menus, cup screens,\n  results and the attract mode do not.\n- **Deep links** (for tests, `tools/check.mjs` and the dev Warp tab): `#title`, `#attract`, `#setup`, `#match`,\n  `#match/<stadiumId>`, `#match/corner`, `#match/freekick`, `#match/penalty`, `#match/shootout`, `#match/super`,\n  `#match/duel`, `#cup`, `#cup/r1`…`#cup/r5` (r1 the Qualifying Round … r5 the final; `#cup/final` works too),\n  `#ending`, `#training/<drillId>`, `#album`, `#about`, `#dev/anim/<move>` (the animation gallery, §11.7). Queries:\n  `?seed=N` (fixes the match RNG), `?touchonly=1` (forces touch-only mode), `?dev=1` (opens the dev panel at start).\n\n### 4.5 Simulation, presentation and network roles\n\n| Role | Runs the Match sim | Reads local input | Draws | Sends |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Single machine (normal) | yes | yes (1–4 players) | yes | — |\n| Hub host with a player | yes | its own player | yes | snapshots and events |\n| Hub host as server (`AIHub.server`) | yes | no | no (draws nothing) | snapshots and events |\n| Hub client | no: it applies snapshots to a mirror Match used for drawing | its own player | yes | its Intent |\n\nThe MatchView and the rig animation code are the same in all four. Only where the state comes from differs.\n\n### 4.6 Registries: the designers' language\n\n`EF.def(kind, id, spec)` registers content; `EF.get(kind, id)` and `EF.all(kind)` read it. Kinds: `team`, `formation`,\n`tactic`, `referee`, `stadium`, `super`, `celebration`, `event`, `drill`, `beat`, `lines` (commentary pools), `chant`,\n`song`, `sticker`, `challenge`, `ball`, `teamtalk`. Content refers to other content by id only. At boot,\n`EF.validate()` checks every reference (a team's stadium, a player's super, a beat's team and photo, a line pool's\nspeaker) and reports problems through `game.fail` (red box). The test suite runs the same check (§11.3). Adding a team,\na stadium or a cup round means adding entries, not code. Appendix C shows each shape.\n\n### 4.7 The Game object\n\n```js\n// first: the Game resolves its view, preset and settings when it is created\nE.VIEWS.broadcast = E.VIEWS.broadcast || new E.View('broadcast', 'Broadcast', 0, 40, 1, 1);   // as kits/pinball.js registers its view\nconst session = !!(window.AIHub && AIHub.active);   // session.js ran first (§3.3), so this is known from the first line\nconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: 'broadcast', bg: '#06070c', id: 'europhoria-football',\n  input: EF.KEYS,\n  corner: !session,                         // the hub has its own corner bar\n  menu: {\n    settings: EF.SETTINGS,                  // §6.31: defined in sessions too (gameplay reads game.settings)\n    controls: { pass: 'Pass / Tackle', shoot: 'Shoot / Slide', lob: 'Lob / Barge', through: 'Through ball / Switch',\n                sprint: 'Sprint', skill: 'Skill move', switch: 'Switch / One-two', dive: 'Theatrics', euphoria: 'Europhoria' },\n    items: session ? [] : [['Restart match', () => EF.restartMatch()], ['Dev mode', () => EF.Dev.open()]],\n    quit: session ? false : 'title', saves: false } });\n```\n\nSaves go through `game.saves` from our own menus, not through the pause menu. Theme the engine's standard HTML screens\n(pause, settings, controls) to match the game with `new E.Shell(game, { theme: { ink: '#fff6e0', paper: '#0d1430',\naccent: '#ffc83a', muted: '#9aa6c8', danger: '#ff5a4a', line: '#2a3a6a' } })`, created once in `00-core.js`.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Engine changes\n\nThe engine is ours to change. Keep every change small and marked `// EF:`, and record each one in\n`engine/CHANGES-ENGINE.md` (what, where, why). Planned changes:\n\n1. **Dialog voices that babble (`E.Dialog`).** In `Dialog.update` the box calls `this.game.audio.sfx(this.voice)` on\n   every second newly shown letter (search for `this._blip++`). Change it so that `voice` may be a function: call\n   `voice(ch, index, line)` once for each newly revealed character (including spaces and punctuation, which the\n   function uses for pauses). Allow `say(text, { voice })` to set the voice for that call only, and restore it after.\n   `EF.babble.voiceFor(speakerId)` returns such a function (§6.22.4).\n2. **Shirt patterns and numbers (`E.Humanoid`).** In the shirt branch of the draw code (search for\n   `const t = tones(C.cloth), plate = [shL, shR`), after the base fill, draw the kit pattern clipped to the torso plate,\n   when `o.kit` is set: `{ pattern: 'hoops' | 'stripes' | 'halves' | 'sash' | 'plain', color2, number, numberColor }`.\n   Build hoops as 3 bands: quads from lerps between the shoulder line (shL to shR) and the hip line (hL to hR) at\n   fractions .2–.33, .5–.63, .8–.93. Build stripes as 3 vertical bands from lerps across the plate, halves as the\n   left half of the plate, and a sash as a diagonal band from shL to hR. Use `px.poly` in `tones(color2).base`, and\n   `.sh` on the side away from the light. Draw the number (1 or 2 digits, `E.font` `'tiny'`, `numberColor`) at the\n   torso centre when the rig faces away from the camera (`!facingCam`). Sleeves take the shirt colour (they already do\n   with `sleeves: 'short'`). Fallback if this proves hard: plain shirts in the main colour with `trim` in `color2`, and\n   no numbers.\n3. **Whole-body tilt (`E.Humanoid`).** A generalisation of the built-in roll (`_roll(u)`, applied when state `flip` is\n   set). Add a state `tilt: { axis: 'forward' | 'right', angle, pivot: 'feet' | 'hips', lift }`. After `solvePose()`,\n   rotate every joint in `J` (except `bladeDir`, which only turns) about the pivot by `angle` around the axis in the\n   rig's frame `[forward, right, up]`:\n   - axis `forward` (a sideways fall: keeper dives, sideways tumbles): `r' = r·cos a + u·sin a`, `u' = −r·sin a + u·cos a`;\n   - axis `right` (pitching forward or back: diving headers, bicycle kicks): `f' = f·cos a − u·sin a`, `u' = f·sin a + u·cos a`;\n\n   then raise the whole body by `lift`, and by however much is needed to keep every joint above the floor (as `_roll`\n   does). Set `this.mocapTilt = { body: M, head: M }` (the 3×3 rotation, rows forward/right/up) for that step so the\n   face, hair and belt details tilt with the body (`_offsets` reads it). Move the cape and hair points for this step\n   only and put them back before they simulate, exactly as `_roll` does with `this._unroll`. Keep the built-in `flip`\n   untouched.\n4. **Named audio buses (`Audio`).** Add `audio.bus(name)`: it returns a `GainNode` (created on first use, connected to\n   the master gain) whose volume follows `game.settings[name]` when that setting exists. It returns null when there is\n   no audio context (headless, or sound blocked). We use buses `voices` and `crowd`. Also add `audio.now()` (the\n   context's `currentTime`, or 0) and expose `audio.noiseBuffer()` (the engine's existing noise buffer), so the crowd\n   engine and the babble voice synthesise with the engine's own context, master volume and mute.\n5. **Accented capitals in the 5×7 font.** Add glyphs for `É È Á Ó Ü Ö Ä Ñ Ç` and lower-case `é è á ó ü ö ä ñ ç`, drawn\n   in the font's style (copy the base letter's bitmap and add the mark in the rows above, for capitals in the\n   ascender space). Names and chants use them (\"OLÉ!\", \"ESTADIO PASAPASA\"). Until this lands, write them in ASCII; the\n   content validator warns about any character the font cannot draw.\n6. **Only if needed:** if the standard `glyphs` setting cannot be redefined from the game (§2.3), set\n   `STANDARD_SETTINGS.glyphs.value` to `'playstation'` and its options to PlayStation and Xbox.\n\nDo not change anything else without adding it to `CHANGES-ENGINE.md`.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Systems and mechanics\n\nUnits are world units (1 tile = 16), seconds and radians. `EF.T.<key>` marks a value tuned live in dev mode\n(Appendix A).\n\n### 6.1 Pitch and stadium geometry\n","numLines":520,"startLine":1,"totalLines":2159}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01TYi87tyk6aGSoxWpeuM72J","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
