{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi8kCDTJ8ZKpkXVd4y9E","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Europhoria Football is built as one self-contained HTML file. The headless test suite passes (103 checks), and so does the AI Hub harness. It has not been played by a person with a real gamepad, so how the controls feel is unjudged.\n\n**Where things are**\n- Game: `EurophoriaFootball-EuropeanFootball-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-073322.html` (1.5 MB), in the work folder root. It runs from `file://`.\n- Exit interview: `exit-interview.json`, same folder. It parses as JSON and every field is answered.\n- Dev folder: `EurophoriaFootball-EuropeanFootball-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-073322/` holds `src/`, the build and test tools, and `engine/CHANGES-ENGINE.md`.\n\n**What the demo contains**\n- **Match:** 5-a-side with slide tackles that send people flying, dives, fouls, advantage, cards, VAR, Euphoria, Europhoria super strikes and the Save Duel.\n- **Modes:** the five-round Cup Run with tabloids, talks, a map and team talks, plus Kick-About for 1–4 players, Penalties, nine training drills, a 51-sticker album and About/Credits.\n- **Required features:** a dev mode (pause menu or F1) with 29 live sliders and a reset to defaults, and attract mode after 10 idle seconds on title and menu screens.\n- **Controller-first UI:** PlayStation glyphs by default with an Xbox option, keyboard and mouse prompts, and mouse hover/click on menus. A touch-only device sees \"Controller or keyboard required\" with a settings button, and the attract mode still runs.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world for 2–4 players.\n- **Audio:** all of it is synthesised in code, with babble voices for the commentators and squad.\n- **Engine changes:** two small ones, recorded in `engine/CHANGES-ENGINE.md`. Dialog voices may be a function, and shirts take a `kit` option (hoops, stripes, halves, sash, plus a number on the back).\n\n**What was checked**\n- `node tools/test.mjs` runs the simulation, scenes, a mock hub and a scripted playthrough of the whole cup to the ending, and prints only failures and a count.\n- The AI Hub harness passed at 2 and 4 players with latency and loss, and in the host-leave and server scenarios.\n- Screenshots, an overhead check and mouse hover/click on the menus passed in headless Chromium.\n\n**Deviations from the plan**\n- Replays turn the camera 32° rather than using several angles.\n- The goalkeeper dives approximate a sideways fall, since the engine has no whole-body tilt.\n- Barry and Trev's commentary is about 250 lines, short of the plan's 300.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 07:33:22\n- **Source:** European football (an over-the-top arcade take on it: terraces, chants, flares, tifos, tactics, dives, VAR, tabloids, commentary cliches, cup magic)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md (Europhoria Football, written by claude-opus-5-5 at max effort). Where it and TASK.md disagreed, TASK.md won, so file and folder names use claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-073322.\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade sports, 5-a-side football with a story-driven five-round Cup Run, 1–4 local players and online versus. A TV broadcast camera, side-on and raised 40° (a custom engine View). Classic (55°) and Aerial (78°) are options in Settings.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (handoff package, full engine, two small marked changes). Plain JavaScript in one IIFE, built by `tools/build.mjs` into one HTML file. AI Hub `session.js` 1.4.0 unmodified. Playwright 1.63.0 and headless Chromium for checks. No other libraries.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native HD pixel-art look at PS1 resolution. Shaded Humanoid rigs in club kits, textured per-stadium pitches with weather and floodlights, code-drawn crests, stands full of fans, tabloid front pages, and pixel-art PlayStation, Xbox, keyboard and mouse glyphs.\n- **Animation:** The engine's Humanoid rig and `E.MOVES` (kick, sweep, bash, poses, flips) driven by each footballer's state. Procedural slides, knockdowns, keeper dives, volleys, bicycle kicks, skill moves and celebrations. No mocap clips.\n- **Audio:** No audio files. Effects, twelve original songs, a noise-bed crowd with team chants, and Animalese-style babble voices are all synthesised in code.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, 2–4 players. The host's copy runs the match and the others send intents and draw 15 Hz snapshots.\n- **Testing:** Headless Node tests behind one command (`node tools/test.mjs`), including a scripted playthrough of the whole cup. The AI Hub harness through Playwright. Screenshots only for what a script cannot check. No subagents or workflows.\n- **Research:** None beyond reading the plan, the engine API card, the sports kit and the hub README.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown (checked in headless Chromium).\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **Issues caught:** About ten real ones. Two set-piece soft-locks: a goalkeeper's goal kick that never released, and a free-kick taker knocked over just before the whistle who never shot. Also a shootout that waited forever on an idle human taker, the match scene overwriting its own `view` property with the MatchView, a nested overlay in the results screen, and several wrong-looking visuals found by screenshot.\n- **Game-breaking:** Three, because a match could never finish: the goal-kick soft-lock, the knocked-down free-kick taker, and the idle shootout taker.\n- **Most helpful test:** The AI-versus-AI soak, which simulates whole matches to full time in seconds and found both soft-locks at once. The scripted whole-cup playthrough then proved the flow from prologue to ending.\n- **Least helpful test:** The half-keyboard mapping and content-reference checks, which passed first time and never failed again.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most:** The headless fixed-step game (`game.advance`, `input.tap`) and the Humanoid rigs. Together with the standard pause, settings and boot screens, a whole match, cup and hub round could be tested without a browser.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong:** `game.go()` cross-fades, so a scene change is not in place after a short `advance`. The scene object is also the game's property bag, so storing a MatchView in `scene.view` silently replaced the view id. `E.Backdrop` is invisible under a ground-view floor.\n- **Most important thing built myself:** The football itself: ball physics with curl, possession, tackles, fouls, VAR, set pieces, shootouts, the Save Duel and the team AI. Then the cup flow and the dev panel.\n- **Hardest part:** Lateral whole-body motion. A Humanoid can only lie along its facing, roll forward or hop, so keeper dives and bicycle kicks are approximations. Finding which audio internals are private was the other friction.\n- **Engine changes:** Two, both marked `// EF:`. `E.Dialog`'s `voice` may be a function so each speaker babbles at their own pitch. `E.Humanoid` takes a `kit` option because clubs are recognised by their shirts.\n- **One change I'd make:** A `tilt` state for Humanoid so keepers can dive sideways without faking it, and a default of no fade for `game.go` in headless games.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most:** The plan was an unusually complete map (folder layout, module list, action table, event names, tuning table, data shapes). TASK.md's controller-first UI, attract mode, dev sliders and hub support gave the demo a clear definition of finished.\n- **Confusing:** The plan named a dev folder and HTML file after claude-opus-5-5-max and a different date, while TASK.md asks for sonnet-5-5-medium and today's stamp. It also says \"follow the plan\" and \"TASK.md wins\" without saying which parts of a 2,000-line plan are must-haves.\n- **Hardest:** Scope. Making every system real at once (rules, referee, VAR, Save Duel, shootouts, replay, co-op, online) without letting them soft-lock each other.\n- **What I'd change:** Mark the plan's features as must, should and could, state the file names once, add per-system done-checks early, and note the headless scene-change fade."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1474,"cache_read_input_tokens":545653,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1474},"output_tokens":792,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"383f0dbc-f521-4dcc-a5c1-4ea066af957b","uuid":"e74c42e4-74ad-4e75-a8bb-3d0d0c27d092","timestamp":"2026-11-04T15:36:06.065Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi8kBssJ98zE3Su5Z6SM"}
