{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi99UJDpEbuuwdvFgWBy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The keeper-dive and bicycle-kick gap is closed. I added a real whole-body tilt to the engine's character rig and drove the dives and kicks from it. Keeper dives now lean sideways, and diving headers and bicycle kicks lean forward or back. A frozen-frame screenshot shows a bicycle-kick body and a diving keeper both visibly tilted. The full suite passed 107 checks, including 4 new tilt checks. I did not re-run it after the last rebuild, which only changed the About text and exit-interview wording.\n\nThe HTML file and `exit-interview.json` are in the work folder root, and the JSON parses. Usage stays \"unknown\" because I have no token or cost figures for this session.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 07:33:22\n- **Source:** European football (an over-the-top arcade take on it: terraces, chants, flares, tifos, tactics, dives, VAR, tabloids, commentary cliches, cup magic)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md (Europhoria Football, written by claude-opus-5-5 at max effort). Where it and TASK.md disagreed, TASK.md won, so file and folder names use claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261104-073322.\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade sports, 5-a-side football with a story-driven five-round Cup Run, 1–4 local players and online versus. A TV broadcast camera, side-on and raised 40° (a custom engine View). Classic (55°) and Aerial (78°) are Settings options. Goal replays cut between three turned angles.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (handoff package, full engine, three small marked changes). Plain JavaScript in one IIFE, built by `tools/build.mjs` into one HTML file. AI Hub `session.js` 1.4.0 unmodified. Playwright 1.63.0 and headless Chromium for checks. No other libraries.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native HD pixel-art look at PS1 resolution. Shaded Humanoid rigs in club kits with hoops, stripes, halves, sashes and shirt numbers. Textured per-stadium pitches with weather and floodlights, code-drawn crests, stands full of fans, tabloid front pages, and pixel-art PlayStation, Xbox, keyboard and mouse glyphs.\n- **Animation:** The engine's Humanoid rig and `E.MOVES` (kick, sweep, bash, poses, flips) driven by each footballer's state. Procedural slides, knockdowns, volleys, skill moves and celebrations. Keeper dives and bicycle kicks use a new whole-body tilt added to the rig. No mocap clips.\n- **Audio:** No audio files. Effects, twelve original songs, a noise-bed crowd with team chants, and Animalese-style babble voices are all synthesised in code.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, 2–4 players. The host's copy runs the match and the others send intents and draw 15 Hz snapshots. It passes the AI Hub harness at 2 and 4 players with latency and loss, and in the host-leave and server scenarios.\n- **Testing:** Headless Node tests behind one command (`node tools/test.mjs`), including a scripted playthrough of the whole cup to the ending. The AI Hub harness through Playwright. Screenshots only for what a script cannot check. No subagents or workflows.\n- **Research:** None beyond reading the plan, the engine API card, the sports kit and the hub README.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown (checked in headless Chromium).\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **Issues caught:** About ten real ones. Two set-piece soft-locks: a goalkeeper's goal kick that never released, and a free-kick taker knocked over just before the whistle who never shot. Also a shootout that waited forever on an idle human taker, the match scene overwriting its own `view` property, a nested overlay in the results screen, and several wrong-looking visuals found by screenshot.\n- **Game-breaking:** Three, because a match could never finish: the goal-kick soft-lock, the knocked-down free-kick taker, and the idle shootout taker.\n- **Most helpful test:** The AI-versus-AI soak, which simulates whole matches to full time in seconds and found both soft-locks at once. The scripted whole-cup playthrough then proved the flow from prologue to ending.\n- **Least helpful test:** The half-keyboard mapping and content-reference checks, which passed first time and never failed again.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most:** The headless fixed-step game (`game.advance`, `input.tap`) and the Humanoid rigs. With the standard pause, settings and boot screens, a whole match, cup and hub round could be tested without a browser.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong:** `game.go()` cross-fades, so a scene change is not in place after a short `advance`. The scene object is also the game's property bag, so storing a MatchView in `scene.view` silently replaced the view id. `E.Backdrop` is invisible under a ground-view floor.\n- **Most important thing built myself:** The football itself: ball physics with curl, possession, tackles, fouls, VAR, set pieces, shootouts, the Save Duel and the team AI. Then the cup flow and the dev panel.\n- **Hardest part:** Lateral whole-body motion. The rig could only lie along its facing, roll forward or hop, so I added a tilt state to the engine myself. Finding which audio internals are private was the other friction.\n- **Engine changes:** Three, all marked `// EF:` and recorded in `engine/CHANGES-ENGINE.md`. `E.Dialog`'s `voice` may be a function (babble per speaker). `E.Humanoid` takes a `kit` option (hoops, stripes, halves, sash, shirt number). `E.Humanoid.update` takes `tilt: { axis, angle, lift }`, a whole-body tilt about the hips.\n- **One change I'd make:** A `game.go(scene, data, { fade: 0 })` default for headless games so tests need not know about the fade, and a documented way to reach the audio context for custom crowd sound.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most:** The plan was an unusually complete map (folder layout, module list, action table, event names, tuning table, data shapes). TASK.md's controller-first UI, attract mode, dev sliders and hub support gave the demo a clear definition of finished.\n- **Confusing:** The plan named a dev folder and HTML file after claude-opus-5-5-max and a different date, while TASK.md asks for sonnet-5-5-medium and today's stamp. It also says \"follow the plan\" and \"TASK.md wins\" without saying which parts of a 2,000-line plan are must-haves.\n- **Hardest:** Scope. Making every system real at once (rules, referee, VAR, Save Duel, shootouts, replay, co-op, online) without letting them soft-lock each other.\n- **What I'd change:** Mark the plan's features as must, should and could, state the file names once, add per-system done-checks early, and note the headless scene-change fade."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":956,"cache_read_input_tokens":573075,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":956},"output_tokens":794,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"383f0dbc-f521-4dcc-a5c1-4ea066af957b","uuid":"cefc42b1-554e-480d-b506-43f1f7b717e6","timestamp":"2026-11-04T15:41:16.463Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi99TwPT3i2KvPUskwh5"}
